  "id": "Compound::DB04861 - Gene::154",
  "verbalization": "Nebivolol, a selective beta-blocker, is shown to bind to Adrb1, a subunit of the beta-adrenergic receptor, which is involved in the regulation of adrenergic signaling. This binding is further linked to the participation of Adrb1 in processes such as Norepinephrine Binding and Epinephrine Binding, both of which are part of the broader adrenergic receptor pathway. These pathways ultimately lead to the participation of Adrb2 in the adrenergic receptor activity, suggesting a potential role for Nebivolol in modulating Adrb2 function. The cumulative evidence from the three paths indicates a consistent and plausible mechanism by which Nebivolol may influence Adrb2, through its interaction with Adrb1 and subsequent downstream signaling. The high scores in the final scores suggest a strong and reliable relationship, supported by multiple independent pathways. The mechanism is biologically plausible, as beta-blockers are known to modulate adrenergic signaling, and the involvement of Adrb2 in this pathway supports the proposed relationship.",
